      "claim": "The belief that flies develop from rotting meat is an example of the doctrine of spontaneous generation.",
      "evidence": "Historical accounts describe spontaneous generation as the idea that life can arise from non‑living matter, e.g., flies from meat.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Spontaneous generation was disproved by systematic experiments (e.g., Redi’s meat‑jar experiments and Pasteur’s swan‑neck flasks).",
      "evidence": "Scientific history records Redi (1668) covering meat to prevent flies and Pasteur (1859) showing no microbial growth in sterile, sealed flasks.",
      "weight": 0.35,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "These disproving studies relied on the method of continued experimentation rather than on a single invention or technique.",
      "evidence": "Both Redi and Pasteur used repeated, controlled experiments to test and refute spontaneous generation.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Among the answer choices, only option (C) \"continued experimentation\" directly matches the historical reason for disproving spontaneous generation.",
      "evidence": "Options (A) atom discovery, (B) better surgical techniques, and (D) microscope invention are unrelated to the key experimental evidence.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "confidence": 0.97,
